// THE OLD VERDICT HERE WAS AN ANNEX C ARGUMENT AND IT IS GONE — the same
// inversion 26_connectmodule_is_not_a_device.va documents one construct down.
// This file used to pin `reject E0201` on the block, reasoning from C.9
// ("Clause 7 only applies to Verilog-AMS HDL") that "a Verilog-A compiler must
// refuse the CONSTRUCT, not merely the keywords". Every quote in that argument
// is real; the conclusion does not follow for a compiler that implements
// Verilog-AMS. Annex C describes the Verilog-A SUBSET — C.9's sentence says
// which clauses bind an AMS implementation, and C.16's list is what the subset
// leaves out — and A.1.2 then leaves an AMS compiler no way to refuse the
// spelling:
//
//     description ::= module_declaration | ... | connectrules_declaration
//     connectrules_declaration ::= connectrules connectrules_identifier ;
//         { connectrules_item } endconnectrules                     // A.1.8
//
// WHAT IS LEFT TO PIN, AND IT IS NOT ABOUT CLAUSE 7. The block below is
// accepted: it parses, and the refusal that remains is about the whole
// source_text rather than about any construct in it. §7.7 makes a
// `connectrules` block configuration FOR the connect module insertion phase —
// which connect modules to insert (§7.7.1), how to resolve candidate
// disciplines (§7.7.2) — so it describes no device of its own, and a
// compilation whose only design element is one has no device to build. §6.2 is
// the clause that says where a device comes from ("the module is the basic
// building block"), so E1001 "source contains no module declaration" is the
// diagnostic, and the reject line below is a CODE precisely so that it cannot
// be confused with the E0201 this file used to demand.
//
// THE CEILING, NAMED, as 26 names its own: a tool that only CHECKED a
// source_text would accept this file outright — A.1.2's `source_text ::=
// { description }` is satisfied by one connectrules block. VerA compiles a
// device, so for VerA "nothing to compile" is a refusal. This file records
// which reason is the honest one, not a claim that the language forbids the
// declaration. (It also means the insertion statement's dangling `a2d` is
// never judged: elaboration returns "no module" before §7.7.1's name check
// (E0915) runs. ch07_mixed_signal/connectrules_accepted.va is where that
// check is exercised, against a device.)
//
// The keyword-as-identifier rules are a different thing again and keep their
// own files: 30_connect_reserved.va, 31_connectrules_reserved.va,
// 16_unused_ams_words_rejected.va. The construct one level down,
// `connectmodule`, is 26; the two stay separate files because neither refusal
// may mask the other.
//
//! lrm 6.2
//! lrm A.1.2
//! reject E1001
connectrules annex_c_connectrules_not_a_device;
    connect a2d;
endconnectrules
